-1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186408103 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "38a55885-c791-5e66-b4d4-e155d750a7c6" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186408103 #edb/ref 1074 "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix, $B$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix, and $C$ is a $3\\times 1$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $3\\times 3$ matrix, which will require finding $9$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $2\\times 1$ matrix, which will require finding $2$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $BC$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $A$ by $BC:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =A(BC) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-14 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 28\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186408104 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186408098 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186408104 #edb/ref 1069 "q-275350" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186408104 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186408104 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $r=⟨-4,-1,2⟩$, $q=⟨-2,3,1⟩$ and $p=⟨5,-2,-1⟩.$ Find $r\\cdot (q\\times p)$ using a determinant.\n\n$r\\cdot (q\\times p)=$\n\n$-21$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186408104 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "40346cc1-7283-5552-9863-224be650a17a" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186408104 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that the scalar triple product $r\\cdot (q\\times p)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $r,$ $q,$ and $p.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot (q\\times p) & =|\\begin{aligned}-4 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-4)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}|-(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}|+2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-4)(3\\cdot (-1)-(-2)\\cdot 1)+((-2)\\cdot (-1)-5\\cdot 1)+2((-2)\\cdot (-2)-5\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =(-4)(-3+2)+(2-5)+2(4-15) \\\\  & =4-3-22 \\\\  & =-21.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186408105 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186408102 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186408105 #edb/ref 1069 "q-96818" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186408105 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186408105 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $g(x)=x?$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186408105 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9af6ee75-bad8-5494-acec-5ee5ed4a4af2" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186408105 #edb/ref 1074 "The notation $g(x)=x$ means that the function $g$ maps the element $x$ in the domain to $x$ in the codomain. This can also be written as\n\n$$\ng:x↦x.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186408106 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186408100 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186408106 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37427" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186408106 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186408106 #edb/ref 1071 "Find all of the subsets of $K={a,c,e}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186408106 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d07d1a60-a504-5d37-bfb6-339fef25fc2f" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186408106 #edb/ref 1074 "To find every subset of $K,$ we list out every combination of elements within $K.$\n\nWe can use the following diagram to visualize the process:\n\n![](images/ae/ae24126292e3dbf82d714e1e6f4f6bd2a63b44de2534e29e4ca9ad9b26fbe308.png)\n\nNote that we must include the empty set since it is a subset of every set.\n\nBy doing this, we get\n\n$$\n∅,{a},{c},{e},{a,c},{a,e},{c,e},{a,c,e}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186408107 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186408113 #edb/ref 175921